A||||4||Pretender||leaking trunks?
Z||000000||Pretender||04-11-99||08:29 PM||||does anyone else have a leaking trunk?..or is this abnormal, i first thought of the temp. of the engine and the condensation from the heat..i did replace the rubber seal around the trunk, should i of put a sealent under the weather-striping??<br>
Z||000001||theogre||04-11-99||10:07 PM||||I'd say if you already did the seal, you have a rust hole some place. look for the center of the wet area if you can. my guess is that's it, or the hole is just above it.<p>mine is dry as a bone, and we've had amazingly wet weather all winter. I've also driven thru some places only a 4WD should go.<p>try pulling the carpet liner. that might make it easier.
Z||000002||Pretender||04-11-99||11:57 PM||||so the trunk walls/floor made of metal?...does the carpet come out easily or is it glued/fastened to the walls?
Z||000003||Patrick||04-12-99||12:18 AM||||My Fiero doesn't have a wing (spoiler?) on the back so I can't have a look, but is there any chance that water is simply getting in where the wing is bolted to the trunk? (Assuming that there's a wing on your Fiero.) I imagine there's supposed to be some kind of a seal in place if the stud goes through to the inside of the trunk. But as we've been reminded a hundred times, these cars are a dozen or so years old. Seals eventually fail.<p>-Patrick
Z||000004||theogre||04-12-99||07:25 AM||||my trunk carpet looks like it is only held up with christmas trees. they just pry out.<p>Yes, the wing, or a rack, could also let water in. In case of a wing, it could even be a cracked trunk lid if the wing took a hit. deck lids with wings should have some more reinforcment added. also makes the wing harder to rip off.<p>whether the trunk is metal, plastic or what ever, you can still have seam split, or crack in it. It tends to get whacked when backing into things that are low to the ground, like curbing.
